Progress
How far along are you?

erebus (formerly x.bin) is production-ready for core use cases and entering Phase 2. The codebase has been fully refactored from the original Python prototype to Rust (v0.5.0): 3 crates — erebus-core (library), erebus-stub (launcher), erebus-cli (builder + dashboard).

Current status:

- 13 runtimes supported (Python, Node.js, Deno, Java, Ruby, .NET, Go, PHP, Perl, native binaries, Hugo, Electron, Wasm, Binary)
- Custom binary format (v2–v5) with SISR delta updates + SquashFS support
- Full security stack: Ed25519 signatures (CVE-2023-48022 compliant), SHA-256 integrity, fail-closed seccomp filtering, user namespace isolation
- Cross-compilation support (musl static-pie, 5 architectures)
- SISR (delta updates) with FastCDC content-defined chunking
- **93.6% bandwidth savings** on ML model updates (231.5 MB → 14.8 MB delta)
- `--gui` flag: bind-mounts X11/Wayland/GPU before pivot_root (unique for desktop ML apps)
- TUI dashboard (`erebus dashboard`) — ratatui-based SISR metrics visualization
- Content-Addressed Storage (CAS) foundation implemented
- Self-hosting: stub packages its own CLI

The core insight: SISR delta updates for large binaries (ML models, Docker images, game assets) are 93% more efficient. We've proven this on a 234 MB GGUF model — users now download only 14.8 MB of changed chunks instead of the full file. The technology applies to any large binary artifact, but ML model distribution is our beachhead ($6.4B MLOps market, Gartner 2025).

Next major milestones:

- Private & public registry (CAS-based, with chunk deduplication)
- GitHub App: auto-build .erebus on tag push → "GitHub Releases for ML models"
- VS Code extension: "Package with erebus" button
- Enterprise air-gap signing workflows

The product is already used by early adopters to package real applications.

How long have each of you been working on this? How much of that has been full-time? Please explain.
Solo founder. Started February 2026, ~6 months of active development (private GitHub repo mid-June 2026 → open-source August 2026, v0.5.0).

The work divides into four phases:

Feb–April: format design, architecture validation, initial Rust launcher (rewrote from Python prototype — the Python was legacy, now fully replaced by 3 Rust crates)

April–June: multi-runtime support (Python → 13 runtimes), security hardening (Ed25519 with bit-set check CVE-2023-48022, seccomp fail-closed, zeroize secrets), CLI polish, test framework

June–July: cross-compilation (5 musl targets), advanced features (OpenTelemetry stub, tree-shake, minify), 402-test suite, documentation, Snap/Flatpak POC

August: SISR delta updates, 16 MiB FastCDC chunks for ML models, TUI dashboard, gstack skill integration (erebus-dashboard in Kilo), YC application

Full-time since February. The pace has been sustainable because of three things:

First, clear architecture upfront. Format v2–v5 design and versioning strategy locked before code started. Security-critical code boundary enforced: erebus-core and erebus-cli have zero unsafe; only erebus-stub has unsafe with SAFETY comments.

Second, AI-assisted development: Claude Code as directed pair programmer + gstack skills for review/testing. Every major decision I reviewed. Several agent defaults I caught and reversed before commit (e.g., it proposed an openssl subprocess dependency; I redirected it to Rust ed25519-dalek instead). Used gstack /plan-ceo-review + /cso (security audit) + /qa for systematic testing.

Third, test discipline: 402 tests (44 core + 52 stub + 306 CLI) written alongside features, not retrofitted. Per-crate clippy with -D warnings enforced in CI.

Normally this takes 6–12 months with a team of 2–3 people. Solo, six months works because multi-runtime support is systematic (one runtime framework, deploy to 14). Security-critical code (launcher, format, signatures) requires careful review, not rapid iteration. And deep systems knowledge (Linux/Rust/binary formats) eliminates false starts that would slow a generalist team.

Full-time commitment is non-negotiable for the next phase: scaling to production (distribution, enterprise features, operations).

